CAT = "arrtmriCare"; SEG = "CAR"
_v1 = 80 * 0.1 / 0.5; assert _v1 == 16.0
_v2 = 70 * 0.1 / 1.0; assert abs(_v2 - 7.0) < 1e-9
_v3 = 25 * 0.1 / 0.5; assert _v3 == 5.0
Q = [
("A patient wants a relative to make health decisions if the patient can no longer speak for themselves. Which document names that person?",
 "Health care proxy", ["Living will", "Do-not-resuscitate order", "Release of information"],
 "A health care proxy (durable power of attorney for health care) names a decision maker. A living will states wishes about treatment but does not appoint anyone."),
("A restless patient keeps moving during a knee scan. Which step stays a positioning aid rather than a restraint?",
 "Pads and a strap the patient can undo", ["Wrist ties fixed to the table rails", "A sheet tucked so the arms cannot move", "A vest strapped under the tabletop pad"],
 "Positioning aids reduce motion with the patient's cooperation and can be released by the patient. Devices that the patient cannot remove limit freedom and count as restraints, which need an order and monitoring."),
("A deaf patient arrives for a brain MRI and reads lips. Which plan keeps communication possible during the scan?",
 "Agree on touch signals before the scan", ["Rely on the intercom between sequences", "Ask a relative to shout into the bore", "Skip the explanation to save some time"],
 "Before entering the bore, set simple signals (a tap on the leg, the squeeze ball) because the patient cannot hear the intercom or see lips inside the magnet."),
("A patient becomes anxious after the table moves into the bore and asks to stop. What should the technologist do first?",
 "Bring the patient out and talk through it", ["Keep scanning and reassure over the intercom", "Give a sedative from the department stock", "Turn off the bore light so the patient can rest"],
 "A patient who asks to stop is taken out of the bore. Coaching, a mirror or prism glasses, or a feet-first position may then help; sedation needs a physician order."),
("A sedated child is monitored during MRI. Which piece of equipment is appropriate in Zone IV?",
 "An MR Conditional oximeter within its limits", ["A standard pulse oximeter with a long cable", "A battery bedside monitor from the ward", "Any small oximeter clipped to the toe"],
 "Monitoring in Zone IV uses equipment labeled MR Conditional, used within its conditions. Ordinary monitors and cables can become projectiles, fail or cause burns."),
("A patient on contact precautions for MRSA is scanned. What is done with the MR table and coils after the exam?",
 "Wipe them with an approved disinfectant", ["Leave them until the end of the day", "Cover them with a fresh sheet only", "Wipe them dry with clean paper towels"],
 "Contact precautions require gloves and gown and disinfection of surfaces the patient touched, using an approved disinfectant at its listed contact time."),
("A patient with pulmonary tuberculosis needs an urgent spine MRI. Which mask does the technologist wear?",
 "A fit-tested N95 respirator", ["A standard surgical mask", "A face shield alone", "No mask if the patient wears one"],
 "Tuberculosis spreads by airborne droplet nuclei, so staff wear an N95 or better respirator. The patient wears a surgical mask when outside an airborne isolation room."),
("Which patient is placed on neutropenic (protective) precautions?",
 "A patient with very few white cells after chemo", ["A patient with influenza and a bad cough", "A patient with a draining wound on the leg", "A child with chickenpox and new blisters"],
 "Protective precautions keep germs away from a patient whose immune defense is weak, the reverse of isolation that keeps a patient's germs from others."),
("Where does a technologist find the hazards and first aid for a disinfectant used on the coils?",
 "The safety data sheet", ["The coil's user manual", "The patient chart", "The DICOM header"],
 "Each hazardous chemical has a safety data sheet listing its hazards, protective equipment, first aid and handling, as OSHA's Hazard Communication Standard requires."),
("An 80 kg patient is to receive a gadolinium agent at 0.1 mmol/kg. The agent is 0.5 mmol/mL. How much is drawn up?",
 "16 mL", ["8 mL", "40 mL", "4 mL"],
 "Dose = 80 kg x 0.1 mmol/kg = 8 mmol; volume = 8 mmol / 0.5 mmol/mL = 16 mL. Stopping at 8 confuses millimoles with milliliters."),
("A 70 kg patient is to receive gadobutrol, a 1.0 mmol/mL agent, at 0.1 mmol/kg. What volume is injected?",
 "7 mL", ["14 mL", "70 mL", "3.5 mL"],
 "Dose = 70 x 0.1 = 7 mmol; at 1.0 mmol/mL that is 7 mL. Gadobutrol is twice as concentrated as the common 0.5 mmol/mL agents, so the volume is half."),
("A 25 kg child is to receive a 0.5 mmol/mL gadolinium agent at 0.1 mmol/kg. What volume is given?",
 "5 mL", ["2.5 mL", "12.5 mL", "10 mL"],
 "Dose = 25 x 0.1 = 2.5 mmol; volume = 2.5 / 0.5 = 5 mL."),
("For an MR angiogram, a small test dose is injected and the time to peak in the vessel is measured before the full bolus. What is this method called?",
 "Timing bolus", ["Bolus tracking", "Fluoro-triggering", "Keyhole imaging"],
 "A timing bolus measures arrival time with a small injection first. Bolus tracking and fluoro-triggering watch the full bolus arrive and start the acquisition then."),
("How do macrocyclic gadolinium agents differ from linear agents?",
 "The ligand rings the ion and binds it tighter", ["They contain no gadolinium ion of any kind", "They are given only by mouth for the bowel", "They shorten T2 but never shorten the T1"],
 "In a macrocyclic agent the ligand forms a ring around the gadolinium ion, which makes the chelate more stable and less likely to release free gadolinium than an open-chain linear agent."),
("A patient feels warmth and stinging at the IV site as a gadolinium injection begins, and the site swells. What should the technologist do first?",
 "Stop the injection", ["Raise the flow rate to finish quickly", "Massage the swelling firmly", "Restart the bolus in the same vein"],
 "Pain and swelling at the site suggest infiltration, so the injection is stopped and the site assessed. Gadolinium volumes are small, so serious injury is rare, but the event is documented and reported."),
("While a patient waits after an outpatient lumbar MRI, the radiologist sees a large spinal epidural abscess. What should happen next?",
 "Keep the patient and alert the care team", ["Discharge the patient as scheduled", "Tell the patient the diagnosis yourself", "Leave it for the routine written report"],
 "A critical finding is communicated promptly to the ordering team under the facility's policy, and the patient is kept for follow-up. Giving a diagnosis is outside the technologist's scope."),
]
